\label{conj:structure of Ordovician lattice}
  The following hold:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Every Ordovician order is a lattice. In particular, every Cambrian lattice is mutable. Moreover, $u(x)$, defined in \cref{the map u}, is obtained from $x$ by a rotation that sends each vertex to the next vertex in the clockwise direction.
    \item Let $L$ be an Ordovician lattice, and let $a,b$ be its atoms. Then $[0,a\vee b]$ is a $(2,m)$-polygon for some $m\geq 2$. Moreover, $L$ is \emph{polygonal}, \text{i.e.}, it satisfies that $[x,y\vee z]$ is a polygon for any $x\prec y,z$ and $[x\wedge y, z]$ is a polygon for any $x,y\prec z$ \text{\cite{MR3645055}}.
    \item Let $L$ be an Ordovician lattice corresponding to $\overrightarrow{B}$, and let $i\in L$ be an atom. Then $\mu_i(L)$ corresponds to the quiver $\mu_i(\overrightarrow{B})$. Here, the correspondence between an Ordovician lattice and a quiver in the above statements is given by \cref{Cambrian lattice and ideal polygon}.
  \end{enumerate}

\label{conj:structure of type-B Ordovician lattice}
